WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.110
Over 400 TV shows are broadcast every year,
and that means that competition for viewers

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:06.110
is fierce.

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:09.950
As a result, networks are often willing to
spend big money on projects they think will

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:11.360
lure in the biggest audience.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.459
They place their bets … and sometimes lose
their shirts.

00:00:14.459 --> 00:00:20.000
Here are some of the most expensive TV flops
to ever have their plugs pulled.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.869
Battlestar Galactica

00:00:21.869 --> 00:00:26.339
Most people today know Battlestar Galactica
as the marathon-worthy sci-fi series that

00:00:26.339 --> 00:00:29.099
ran from 2003 to 2009.

00:00:29.099 --> 00:00:34.050
Hardcore fans, however, might remember that
the series originated in 1978… and tanked

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:35.620
in the ratings a year later.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:40.870
Setting out to be a TV take on Star Wars,
the original Galactica cost roughly $1 million

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:45.540
per episode—more than double that of a typical
prime-time show at the time, and the equivalent

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:47.770
of over 3.7 million today.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.140
At first, it was worth the price tag.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:54.340
The series' early episodes attracted positive
reviews and healthy ratings, but those ratings

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:59.489
eventually began to slip, and behind-the-scenes
issues like censorship made production difficult.

00:00:59.489 --> 00:01:04.070
Ultimately, Battlestar Galactica couldn't
pull its weight as compared to other less-expensive

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:09.230
network hits such as ABC's own Mork &amp; Mindy,
and the show was canceled after a single season.

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:14.490
The network did give it another go with Galactica
1980, but that show only lasted ten episodes

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:19.240
before being decommissioned due to nonexistent
viewers.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:20.240
Supertrain

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:25.079
When it comes to expensive flops, you'd be
hard-pressed to find a bigger trainwreck than

00:01:25.079 --> 00:01:27.619
1979's Supertrain.

00:01:27.619 --> 00:01:32.979
As you might expect from the title, the NBC
show was basically just Love Boat meets Snowpiercer,

00:01:32.979 --> 00:01:38.079
set on a massive, nuclear-powered locomotive
featuring its own nightclubs and casinos that

00:01:38.079 --> 00:01:41.229
could speed from New York to Los Angeles at
200 miles per hour.

00:01:41.229 --> 00:01:46.380
The thing is, the train didn't actually exist,
so the show’s creators used expensive model

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:47.750
trains while filming.

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:51.700
Unfortunately, the models weren't particularly
durable, and one of them was destroyed in

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.500
a crash before the first episode was even
filmed.

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:58.810
After the show debuted to downright terrible
ratings, famous celebrities were brought on

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:00.500
to keep Supertrain on track.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:06.079
Tony Danza, Rue McClanahan, and Dick van Dyke
all made guest appearances, but even that

00:02:06.079 --> 00:02:10.230
late ‘70s star power couldn’t stop Supertrain
from going off the rails.

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:15.090
The show was canceled after nine episodes
and remains one of NBC's costliest flops to

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:19.230
date at a reported $4 million an episode.

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:20.960
Father of the Pride

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:25.810
It makes a lot of sense that after Shrek raked
it in at the box office, NBC was willing to

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:30.300
fork over big bucks to Dreamworks for a computer-animated
show of their own.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:34.750
It makes a lot less sense that they decided
that the project that would be worth $2.5

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:39.320
million of their money would be a show that
recast the white lions from Siegfried and

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:43.200
Roy’s famous Las Vegas magic act as a wacky
sitcom family.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.270
The result was Father of the Pride.

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:49.980
Unlike Shrek, which somehow spawned an unstoppable
franchise that we just can’t seem to get

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.690
rid of, it failed to capture the hearts and
minds of those who watched it.

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:56.870
It did, however, capture plenty of unwanted
attention.

00:02:56.870 --> 00:03:01.570
The Parents Television Council flooded the
FCC with allegations that Father of the Pride

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:02.570
was indecent.

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:06.650
To make matters worse, the show came in hot
on the heels of Roy being nearly mauled to

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:10.950
death when one of his tigers dragged him offstage
by the neck, the first such accident in more

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:12.950
than 5,000 shows.

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:17.100
With all that stacked against it, it didn’t
take long for NBC to make the show disappear

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:20.050
after only 13 episodes.

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:21.670
Viva Laughlin

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:25.370
When some shows get canceled, they leave viewers
feeling like they missed out on something

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:27.550
good that never got a chance to shine.

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:32.440
When CBS dropped the hammer on 2007's Viva
Laughlin, however, it was a mercy killing

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:35.920
that freed the audience from what the New
York Times suggested might be “the worst

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:37.800
show in the history of television.”

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:42.750
Made in an effort to diverge from the standard
lineup of police procedurals and grandparent-focused

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:49.090
sitcoms, CBS banked big on a remake of Viva
Blackpool, a popular BBC show with a bizarre

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:53.340
premise: a murder mystery that would occasionally
break out into elaborate musical numbers where

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:55.200
the cast sang pop songs.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:02.390
[Singing] “I'm still standing bigger than
I ever did/lookin' like a true survivor!"

00:04:02.390 --> 00:04:06.620
Despite the presence of producer and star
Hugh Jackman, critics and viewers alike hated

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:09.520
the casino-themed musical from the opening
number.

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:14.060
Its ratings were dismal, and it quickly became
a go-to punchline on the E! Network’s The

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:18.700
Soup as a show “beloved by hundreds,”
which might’ve been overstating it.

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:24.400
After only two episodes, Viva Laughlin danced
its way off airwaves, and with the pilot purportedly

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:30.800
costing $6.8 million, cutting losses early
was probably the right call.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:31.850
Camelot

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:37.200
As the first series ordered under Chris Albrecht,
then newly crowned president and CEO of Starz,

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:41.090
hopes were riding high on 2011's Arthurian
drama, Camelot.

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:45.660
According to Albrecht, the show was based
on, quote, “an idea of hope that has resonated

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:47.970
at different times throughout history."

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:51.450
Unfortunately, 2011 was not one of those times.

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:55.380
Critical reviews of Camelot were less than
stellar, but while the show still saw solid

00:04:55.380 --> 00:05:00.790
ratings during its final episodes, Starz canceled
the fantasy-drama after only one season.

00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:05.610
Scheduling conflicts with lead cast members
Joseph Fiennes, Jamie Campbell Bower, and

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:09.290
Eva Green made a sophomore effort more trouble
than it was worth.

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:10.940
And it was worth quite a lot.

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:15.860
According to The Wall Street Journal, the
series cost roughly $7 million per episode.

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:17.730
That’s a Lance-a-lot of money, huh?

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:21.750
Look, you can’t blame us for trying that
one.

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:22.830
Kings

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:27.230
Betting on the star-power of Deadwood's Ian
McShane, the writing prowess of American Gods'

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:31.340
Michael Green, and some assistance from The
Hunger Games: Catching Fire director Francis

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:37.650
Lawrence, NBC seemed poised to hit it big
with 2009's Kings, an ambitious drama reimagining

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:40.950
the life of the biblical King David in a modern
setting.

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:46.550
The two-hour premiere cost a massive $10 million,
with each subsequent episode coming in at

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:47.900
$4 million a pop.

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:51.490
With that kind of money behind a television
series, you better believe the stakes were

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:52.490
high.

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:56.400
In this case, producers rolled snake eyes
and cashed out with dismal ratings.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.530
But Kings wasn't just costly for NBC.

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:04.920
Liberty Mutual invested a $5 million sponsorship
into the show, reportedly aiming to subconsciously

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:10.040
piggyback on the show's message of personal
responsibility to encourage viewers to buy

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:11.120
insurance.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:15.790
With barely anyone watching the show, however,
it's safe to assume Liberty Mutual didn't

00:06:15.790 --> 00:06:17.580
exactly get what it paid for.

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:22.110
Kings was first banished to the dog days of
summer before NBC officially called it quits.

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:26.260
Still, the show isn't as bad as its ratings
imply, and it might be worth going back if

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:28.930
you missed it the first time around.

00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:31.160
Marco Polo

00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:36.120
Netflix is no stranger to dropping big money
on big projects, but in the case of 2014's

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:40.750
Marco Polo, the series never really found
its way to an audience, and wound up losing

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:41.750
big in the process.

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:47.050
At the time of Marco Polo's release, no active
series was more expensive to produce, except

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:49.590
HBO's fantasy-juggernaut Game of Thrones.

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:55.130
The first season of Netflix's Kublai Khan-centered
drama cost a staggering $90 million, which

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:57.730
weighs in at $9 million per episode.

00:06:57.730 --> 00:07:02.470
Unfortunately for Netflix, Marco Polo never
came close to Game of Thrones' success.

00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:07.490
Despite lackluster critical reviews, Netflix
gave Marco Polo a second season.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:11.770
The sophomore effort was less popular than
the first, prompting the streaming service

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:13.410
to give the series the axe.

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:16.500
(In this case, a 13th-century Mongol axe.)

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:18.100
[Thud, scream]

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:19.270
Thanks for watching!

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:21.750
